# Local development
# connection_limit is not optional on a small server. Prisma's default pool is
# (cpus * 2 + 1) PER PROCESS, and a Next build runs several workers at once —
# on a 4-core box that is 9 connections each, which exhausts Postgres mid-build
# and fails the deploy. Five per process leaves room for both apps and psql.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://fantazia:fantazia@127.0.0.1:5432/fantazia?schema=public&connection_limit=5"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="http://localhost:3000"
AUTH_SECRET="dev-only-change-me-in-production-min-32-chars"
# MEDIA_ROOT — where uploads are written. MUST be absolute: both apps read it,
# and a relative path resolves against each app's own working directory.
# On cPanel, point this at a directory under public_html so the web server
# serves the files directly and Node stays out of the request path.
MEDIA_ROOT="/var/www/fantazia/media"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_URL_BASE — set when the web server serves MEDIA_ROOT directly.
# Leave unset in development; the apps stream from /media themselves.

# Lets the admin clear the public site's cache immediately after publishing,
# instead of waiting out the ISR window. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
REVALIDATE_SECRET="change-me"
# Where the admin reaches the public app internally.
WEB_URL="http://127.0.0.1:3000"
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""

# ---------- Payments ----------
# No provider has been chosen yet. "test" is a stand-in that always succeeds
# when you press Pay, and it REFUSES to run when NODE_ENV=production unless you
# also set ALLOW_TEST_PAYMENTS=yes — a payment page that always succeeds must
# never be reachable on a live site by accident.
#
# To go live: implement PaymentProvider in packages/booking/src/payment.ts for
# the provider you pick (Paymob, Stripe, whichever), set PAYMENT_PROVIDER to
# its name, and point its webhook at /api/payments/webhook. Nothing else in the
# system changes.
PAYMENT_PROVIDER="test"
PAYMENT_TEST_SECRET="change-me-in-any-shared-environment"
# ALLOW_TEST_PAYMENTS="yes"   # staging only, never production

# ---------- OPERA / OWS ----------
# The credentialRef on an IntegrationEnvironment row names these, prefixed.
# For credentialRef "OPERA_PROD" the server reads OPERA_PROD_USERNAME and so on.
# Namespaces and SOAP actions come from the WSDL your PMS team exports — see
# docs/opera-provisioning-runbook.md Stage 2. The defaults in code are
# placeholders and will not work against a real installation.
# OPERA_PROD_USERNAME=""
# OPERA_PROD_PASSWORD=""
# OPERA_PROD_NS_RESERVATION=""
# OPERA_PROD_ACTION_CREATE=""
# OPERA_PROD_ACTION_FETCH=""
# OPERA_PROD_ACTION_CANCEL=""
# OPERA_PROD_TIMEOUT_MS="20000"

# ---------- Email ----------
# SMTP, because cPanel already provides it with the domain's SPF and DKIM
# already in place. Create a mail account (e.g. reservations@) in cPanel and
# put its details here. With these unset, messages queue in the outbox and wait
# rather than failing — nothing is lost, but nothing reaches a guest either.
SMTP_HOST=""
SMTP_PORT="587"          # 465 for implicit TLS, 587 for STARTTLS
SMTP_USER=""
SMTP_PASSWORD=""
SMTP_FROM="reservations@fantaziahotels.com"
# SMTP_REPLY_TO=""       # defaults to SMTP_FROM
RESERVATIONS_EMAIL="reservations@fantaziahotels.com"   # shown to guests as the contact

# ---------- Worker ----------
# WORKER_INTERVAL_MS="60000"
